<% content_for :title, "SearchOps crawler" %>

<section class="so-container py-14 sm:py-18" aria-labelledby="crawler-title">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="so-eyebrow">Crawler identification</p>
    <h1 id="crawler-title" class="mt-2 text-4xl font-black tracking-tight text-slate-950">About SearchOpsBot</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8 text-slate-700">SearchOpsBot performs bounded technical audits requested by SearchOps customers for properties they configure. Its robots product token is <code>SearchOpsBot</code>.</p>

    <section class="so-card mt-8 p-6" aria-labelledby="request-identity-title">
      <h2 id="request-identity-title" class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-950">HTTP request identity</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-slate-700">Crawler requests use this honest identification string:</p>
      <code class="mt-3 block overflow-x-auto rounded-lg bg-slate-950 p-4 text-sm text-white"><%= @crawler_user_agent %></code>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8" aria-labelledby="robots-policy-title">
      <h2 id="robots-policy-title" class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-950">Robots policy and security</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-700">SearchOps respects robots.txt by default. An eligible customer with current verified ownership can explicitly override robots rules for its own audit, but that never bypasses destination validation, public-network restrictions, crawl scope, rate limits, quotas or authentication.</p>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-slate-700"><strong>robots.txt is not access control.</strong> Its paths are publicly visible requests to crawlers, not proof that content is private or absent from search results. Protect sensitive resources with real application-layer authorization.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</section>
